    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. Here's what I changed to implement the spec:\n\n## Change\n\nMembership level 4 is now defined as requiring tenure of more than 365 days since the owner's `registrationDate`, and the cap is raised from 3 to 4.\n\n**`MembershipLevel.java`** \u2014 the `of(...)` method now takes the owner's `registrationDate` as a third argument. It still awards +1 for a non-blank email and +1 for `namesakeCount == 0`, then awards a further +1 when `ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(registrationDate, LocalDate.now()) > 365`, capped at 4. Because a newly created owner's registration date is the current business day, its tenure is 0, so it can never earn the tenure point \u2014 a new owner with an email and no namesake maxes the pre-tenure factors at **level 3**, not 4. Updated the class Javadoc accordingly.\n\n**Callers updated to the new signature:**\n- `OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 the `membershipLevel` mapping now passes `owner.getRegistrationDate()`.\n- `AuditOwnerCreated.java` \u2014 the audit log call now passes the registration date too.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` passes (a fresh owner with an email and no namesake resolves to `membershipLevel: 3`), and the log confirms it: `Owner created: ... membershipLevel=3`.\n\nNote: the spec's parenthetical mentions \"a 3-member household\" as flavor for maxing the pre-tenure factors, but household size isn't among the level's inputs in this codebase (level derives from email + namesake count + tenure), so I left the factor set unchanged and only added the tenure rule.",
